  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1120 PM MDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nThe cutoff low off the Southern California Coast is picking up the \nremnants of Rachel as it ejects to the northeast in the \nsouthwesterly flow driven by the ridging over texas and the trough \ndigging into the Great Basin. The showers and thunderstorms this \nafternoon have stratified out to light rain across eastern Utah and \nWestern Colorado. Satellite imagery shows jet winds aloft running 80 \nto 100 kts at H250, more in line with the Euro model. The 00Z \nsoundings showed deep moisture across northern Arizona extending to \nthe northeast into Western Colorado. Models are in good agreement \nwith the Pacific Northwest low continuing to descend south through \nthe Great Basin Monday night before ejecting to the northeast across \nthe region Tuesday into Wednesday. This pattern brings continued \nrain across much of the region tomorrow into Monday. Guidance is \nkeeping the heaviest precipitation over the Uintas and Tavaputs at \ntwo to three inches and one to two inches over the terrain to the \nsouth and east with the lower valleys only seeing another quarter \ninch or so. The most significant weather will be the stronger winds \nacross the region tomorrow afternoon and evening across the south \nwith winds continuing through the overnight in the northern areas. A \nwind advisory is in effect for the Four Corners area and in the Moab \narea for winds 25 to 35 mph gusting to 50 mph. Elsewhere, look for \nwindy conditions gusting to 35-40 mph in the lower valleys and 40-50 \nmph over the higher terrain. Though these winds will taper off in \nthe late evening down south, they will pick up across the northern \nareas through the evening before tapering off in the overnight. \nThere is uncertainty as to whether the skies will clear out in the \nmorning to allow heating for stronger thunderstorms through the day, \nor remain cloudy to limit the stronger thunderstorms. With the \nstrong flow aloft, any convection will be capable of mix the upper \nlevel winds down to the surface producing locally strong winds in \nexcess of 60 mph.",
